window.tuataraManualText = {"01-what-palimpsest-is":" what palimpsest is a package that takes a pdf apart, save by save, and looks for signs of editing. a palimpsest is a page that was written over: the old writing is scraped off, and it still shows under the new. a pdf is often like that. when a program changes a finished pdf it can add the change at the end of the file and leave what was there before, so the earlier text is still in the file. other edits leave other traces: a date that cannot be right, a white box over a figure, a character copied over another in a scan. palimpsest reads a pdf, or a picture of a scanned page, and reports each save the file holds and every such trace. it is a package, not part of the editor, and it is for people who must judge whether a document is what it says it is. this chapter says how to load it, what it reads, what it never does, and how much a finding can be trusted. the chapters after it cover each part of the report and each check.","01-what-palimpsest-is#loading-it":"1.1 loading it palimpsest is loaded like any package, with a line in your init file: (load-package \"palimpsest\" :source \"https://github.com/tuatara-editor/tuatara-palimpsest\") save the file and start tuatara again. tuatara fetches the package the first time, offers to show you its source and asks whether to compile and load it. answer y . after that it loads at every start. chapter 19 of the main manual explains packages, signatures and updates. to read scanned pages palimpsest needs three programs on the machine: bubblewrap, imagemagick and poppler. on fedora they are in the packages bubblewrap , imagemagick and poppler-utils . a pdf with no scanned page needs none of them.","01-what-palimpsest-is#what-it-reads":"1.2 what it reads palimpsest reads the octets of the file, with its own code. octets are the bytes of the file. it does not hand the pdf to another program to open, and it follows the pdf's own structure: the cross-reference sections, which say where each object begins, the trailers, the objects and their streams, the pages and what they draw. it examines two kinds of file: a pdf, taken apart into its saves, which the report calls revisions; a picture file (png, jpeg or tiff), examined as one scanned page. a picture has no revisions, so the commands that write or show a revision say so.","01-what-palimpsest-is#what-it-never-does":"1.3 what it never does nothing in a file is run. javascript, actions that launch programs and addresses to visit are named in the report and never followed. nothing the file names is fetched. the file is not changed, and nothing is written beside it: what palimpsest writes goes to a directory of its own in tuatara's cache directory, readable by you alone. a directory named in *evidence-directories* refuses every write, as the main manual's chapter 12 says, so nothing palimpsest makes can land in one. pictures in a scanned page are decoded by programs shut in a sandbox, a wall that lets a program read what it is given and write nowhere else. a page kept as fax or jbig2 is drawn by poppler's pdftoppm in the sandbox, from a pdf of that picture alone. only the pictures of scanned pages are decoded; no page is ever drawn as a picture to be compared. what the report quotes from the file, such as a producer, a signer's name or text from a page, is written with each control character shown as tuatara shows it on the screen, an escape as ^[ and a bell as ^g . reading a kept report with cat in a terminal cannot move the cursor or hide a line, whatever was written into the pdf.","01-what-palimpsest-is#a-sign-is-not-proof":"1.4 a sign is not proof every finding says that something in the file looks like a trace of editing. none of them says who edited the file, or that the edit was wrong. honest files have later saves, programs that edit pdfs, fonts in two subsets, scans with uneven ink. each finding names the honest causes it knows, so that you can weigh them. chapter 6 says how the strengths are given and how to read a finding. two things palimpsest cannot do, and you must not expect of it: find an earlier save that was erased without trace. a file written again in one go, with nothing of the old text in it, has no earlier save to read; say that a file is genuine. \"no sign of editing found\" means that none of its checks found one, and a careful hand leaves few. chapter 9 lists the other limits, and chapter 7 what each check can and cannot tell. try it, loading palimpsest and opening a report add the load-package line above to your init file with c-x c-f on ~/.config/tuatara/init.lisp , and save it with c-x c-s . restart tuatara. to the question about compiling and loading palimpsest, press v to read its source, q to come back, then y . open a pdf of your own with c-x c-f . run m-x palimpsest . a buffer named after the file opens and says it is examining. in a moment the report replaces that text. chapter 2 reads it.","02-examining-a-file":" examining a file running palimpsest on a pdf or a picture, and reading the report it makes. m-x palimpsest examines one file and shows the result in a buffer of its own. the report is long on purpose: it says what was found, where, and how to see it again by hand. this chapter covers how to start an examination and what each part of the report holds. chapter 3 goes on to the revisions and objects the report links to.","02-examining-a-file#starting-an-examination":"2.1 starting an examination palimpsest examines the file the current buffer shows, when its octets are a pdf: a pdf's page view, a pdf visited as text, the pdf on a line of rimu or of the directory editor. when the buffer is not one of those, or always with c-u , it asks pdf to examine: . a picture file (png, jpeg or tiff) is examined too, as one scanned page. it refuses what is not a file, and what does not begin as a pdf does (the file does not begin with %pdf- ). the work runs in the background, as a job, and the editor never waits. the letter p is lit in the report's mode line meanwhile. c-x j lists the job, examining name for signs of editing , with what it is doing and how far it has got. c-x j stops it, at once, even in the middle of a page that draws millions of operators. the report is a buffer of its own, *palimpsest: name* , read only. a file is examined once while its report is open: palimpsest on it again shows the report as it was. to examine a file again after it has changed, close its report first with c-x k . modal non-modal command what it does none m-x palimpsest palimpsest examine a pdf, or a picture (png, jpeg or tiff), for signs of editing, from its octets alone.","02-examining-a-file#the-parts-of-the-report":"2.2 the parts of the report a report has a title, a line saying that nothing in the file was run and the file was not changed, and then these parts: table 2.1. the parts of a report. part what it holds * summary the file, its size, sum and dates, and the verdict. * checks a row for each check run, with what it found. * findings every sign found, strongest first. * methods the ways of editing the findings tell of, with the craft each takes. * fingerprint what tells who edited the file and with what. * revisions one part for each save, from the original to the latest. * saved added once you have saved the work: where each copy is. a picture file has no * revisions part. tab goes to the next link in the report and s-tab to the one before. ret on a link follows it.","02-examining-a-file#the-summary":"2.2.1 the summary table 2.2. the lines of the summary. line what it says file where the pdf is. size its size in octets, in hex, and in kb or mb. sha-256 the sum of the file as it was read. the same file always gives the same sum and any other file another, so it names the evidence exactly. pdf version what its header says, or not given when no %pdf- is in its first 1,024 octets. revisions how many saves it holds: the original, or the original and so many later saves. pages how many pages the latest save has. verdict see below. findings how many findings of each strength. made , changed the latest save's /creationdate and /moddate , left out when it gives none. saved to where the work was kept, once you have saved it. creator , the latest save's /creator and /producer . producer not read what palimpsest could not read, a line for each. the verdict weighs only the strengths of the findings. it reads strong signs that the file was edited after it was made. when any finding is strong, signs worth a look, none of them strong. when the strongest are worth a look, and no sign of editing found. otherwise. read the findings before you rely on it. not read lines keep a report from seeming more complete than it is. they say that the file does not begin with %pdf- in its first 1,024 octets; that it has no %%eof line, so its objects were read as one save; that it holds more objects, saves or decoded data than the limits of chapter 9 allow, so the rest were not read; or that a page's content stream could not be read, naming the revision and page and the cause, such as it is encrypted . a page that cannot be set beside the save before is named too. the rest of the examination goes on: one page never ends it. once any save names /encrypt , every string and stream of the file is taken as encrypted, those written before that save too, as a pdf reader takes them. they are not decrypted, so the content of such a file's pages, fonts and pictures is not read. how the file is put together, its saves and its cross-reference sections still are.","02-examining-a-file#the-checks":"2.2.2 the checks * checks has a row for each check that runs on this kind of file. the row's name is a link: ret on it opens, in a help buffer, what the check looks for, how it finds it and what honest files can show alike. the rest of the row says how the check went: the strongest finding it made, with what it counts (characters, regions, patches, else findings) and the pages; none , when it ran and found nothing; not run , with the reason ( no scanned pages , no text layer , no pictures , not a jpeg , no pages ), when the file has nothing for the check to read. such a check says nothing either way; could not finish , when it failed on what the file holds. a finding for the record says why. chapter 7 lists the checks.","02-examining-a-file#the-findings":"2.2.3 the findings under * findings the findings are grouped by strength, the strongest first, each group headed with how many it holds: ** strong signs of editing (2) , ** worth a look (4) , ** for the record (1) . within a group they go by revision, then page. a finding about the whole file is put in the latest revision. each finding is a line beginning with - , which says what was found and what it means in one sentence. a strong one is shown in the colour of warnings. under it come: where: the revision, the page when the finding is about one, and the objects it concerns. each number is a link, and ret on it shows the object as that revision has it (chapter 3). past 20 of them it says and n more ; its details when it has any: the text before and after, what is drawn, the programs named, each on a line of its own. the logs of chapter 3 hold more for each finding: the rule that found it, the evidence and the steps to see it again by hand. when a check fails on a damaged file, the others still run, and the failure becomes a finding of its own, for the record: the check of name could not finish on this file: why .","02-examining-a-file#methods-and-fingerprint":"2.2.4 methods and fingerprint each finding names the way of editing it plausibly comes from: a white box over text, figures retyped in another font, characters cut out of a scan. * methods lists those ways, the most practised first, each with what shows it and the findings that do. above them a line gives the craft they take, on four levels: table 2.3. the craft of editing. level craft what it takes 1 plain what anyone does with a pdf editor or a picture editor's text tool. 2 practised matching what shows: fonts, sizes, places, the file's own account of itself. 3 skilled work in a scan's pixels, or round the file's structure and its signatures. 4 expert skilled work with its traces covered as well, such as a scan saved again to blur them. the levels rate what was found, never what was not. a careful hand leaves fewer signs, so a file that shows level 1 may have been worked on with more craft. a way shown only by signs worth a look is marked (signs worth a look only) . when the highest level rests on such signs alone, the craft line says so and gives the level the strong signs show. when none of the signs is strong, it says an honest file can show as much. * fingerprint holds what tells of who edited the file and with what: method : each way of editing found; editor : each program a later save names that the original does not; made-by : the programs the original names, which made the document and did not edit it; index : how a later save keeps its cross-references, as a table or a stream; jpeg : the quantisation tables each jpeg picture was saved with, by a short name of their own and the quality they stand for. a scanner's software saves with its tables. someone who opens a scan and saves it again saves with their editor's, and those stay with the file. s writes the fingerprint to a file of its own, which chapter 5 uses to group files into cases. try it, reading a report examine a pdf of your own with m-x palimpsest . read the * summary . note the verdict and the line findings . press tab until the cursor is on a name in * checks , and press ret . the help buffer says what that check looks for. press q to close it. search for a finding with c-s and read its revision, page and objects. press c-x k to close the report. if you have not saved its copies it asks first.","03-revisions-and-objects":" revisions and objects reading each save of the file, looking at its objects, and keeping the work. a pdf that was changed after it was made often holds the earlier versions in itself. each save is a revision. palimpsest lists every revision, shows any object of any revision, writes each revision out as a pdf of its own, and writes a log for each that another examiner can follow with a hex editor and nothing else. this chapter covers those parts of the report and the keys that use them.","03-revisions-and-objects#the-revisions":"3.1 the revisions under * revisions each save has a part of its own, from the original to the latest. the heading gives the revision's number, with (the original) for the first and (the current file) for the last, its first and last octet in the file and its size. table 3.1. the lines of a revision. line what it says written as the revision written out as a pdf of its own, and its log, each a link; or why it was not written. index its cross-reference section: a table, a stream, a table and a stream (hybrid), or none, the objects then found by scanning; where the section is and where its startxref is. trailer its catalogue ( /root ), its document information ( /info ), its /id in hex and its /prev , where the section before is. claims when this save's document information says the document was made and last changed, and by which program. adds the objects it adds, each a link. changes the objects it writes again with other octets. frees the objects it frees. their links show them as the save before had them. not right what makes no sense in its cross-reference section or trailer, a line for each. pages the pages this save touched: those that draw with an object it adds or changes, and pages it adds. page n, its text: the lines of the page's text that differ from the save before, - for gone and + for new, 60 at most. page n, what it draws: what the page draws now that it did not ( + ) and what it drew and no longer does ( - ), 40 of each at most. =page n of the revision a page this save removed. before is gone.= objects written again with the very same octets are not listed here. the log lists them. two pages are set beside each other when they are the same page object, the same number, in both saves, or else when they are at the same place. two things drawn are the same when they are of the same kind and text, at the same place and size to the nearest point, of the same colour to a twentieth and in the same render mode. so text moved by a point or recoloured slightly shows as gone and new. what is laid over a page is set beside the save before too: each annotation gets a line with its kind, what it says, a form field's name and value, a shape's fill, where it lies and whether it is hidden, shown under \"laid over\". a save that only lays a text box and a stamp over a total, the page's own operators untouched, shows them there as added.","03-revisions-and-objects#showing-an-object":"3.2 showing an object any object can be shown, read only, in a buffer of its own: ret on an object's number in the report shows it as the revision it is listed under has it; o asks object number (n): , offering the number at the cursor. it shows the object as the revision whose part of the report the cursor is in has it, and anywhere else as the latest save has it. which revision the cursor is in is told by the report's own headings, never by anything the pdf says; c-u o does the same and shows a stream in hex whatever it holds. modal non-modal command what it does none o , c-c p o palimpsest-show-object show an object of the pdf the report is on, read only: its place in the file, its dictionary as written and its stream decoded (at most palimpsest-largest-shown ), as text, or in hex in a buffer of its own when it is not text or with c-u. the object's buffer, *palimpsest: name object n, revision r* , says: where the object is: where it begins and ends and the revision that wrote it. an object packed in an object stream is placed by the stream and the offset of its decoded octets. it is not in this revision. means it was freed or not yet made; what it is to the document, when that is known: a page's content, the catalogue, the page tree, the document information, the xmp metadata, the form, a font, a picture, an annotation, a form field. otherwise what its /type or /subtype says; its dictionary as written, octet for octet, up to 64 kb; for a stream: its size in the file, where its data is, its filters, and how much it decoded to, or why it was not decoded ( picture data (/dctdecode), not decoded , it is encrypted ). then come the decoded octets as text, or, when they are not text, a line in hex: and the name of a buffer that shows them in the hex view, shown at once. at most *palimpsest-largest-shown* octets of a stream are shown (1 mb as standard), and the rest is counted.","03-revisions-and-objects#the-logs":"3.3 the logs for each revision palimpsest writes a log, revision-n.log , beside the revision's pdf. ret on its name in the report opens it, read only, and q closes it. it is written so that someone else can do it all again by hand. its parts: its head: the source file's name, size and sha-256, when the log was written and by which version of palimpsest; how the revision was found: where its %%eof is, where its startxref is and the offset it names, the kind and place of its cross-reference section, its trailer in hex and as text, its /prev , anything not as it should be, the revision's octets, the sha-256 of revision-n.pdf and how to check it: copy the source file's first n octets into a new file and compare sums; the signs found in this revision, each numbered with its strength, words, page and objects and then the rule that found it in one sentence, the details , the evidence (offsets in hex and decimal, with the octets in hex and as text, a full stop standing for an octet that is no printable character) and the steps to see it by hand ; the objects: what the revision adds, changes, writes again unchanged and frees, with where each is; the changes against the revision before: for each page the text lines gone and new, the annotations gone and new, and each thing drawn now and before, with the operators that draw it and their place in the decoded content stream. the same file always gives the same log, but for the line saying when it was written, so two examiners' logs can be compared. an offset in the decoded octets of a stream counts from the first octet of the decoded stream. for a stream with no filter that is the stream's octets in the file.","03-revisions-and-objects#keeping-the-work":"3.4 keeping the work when a report is made, each revision is written out as revision-n.pdf : the file's octets from its start to the end of that revision's %%eof line, the document as it stood after that save. the log goes beside it. they go to a directory of the report's own, under palimpsest/ in tuatara's cache directory, with the files read only. nothing is written beside the pdf examined. that directory belongs to the report. closing the report deletes it with what is in it, and one left behind when tuatara ended without closing the report is deleted at the next report after a day. to keep the work, save it. s saves everything: every revision, its log, the report as name-revision-report.txt and the fingerprint as name-fingerprint.txt , to a directory you name. the directory is made when it is not there. the copying runs in the background and each copy's sha-256 is checked against its original. the report ends with a part * saved saying where each copy is, the summary gains a line saved to , and closing the report no longer asks. modal non-modal command what it does none s , c-c p s palimpsest-save save the revisions of the pdf the report is on, each with its log, and the report itself as name-revision-report.txt, to a directory asked for (made when it is not there): the work to keep, as the report's own copies go when it is closed. saving is refused for: the directory of the pdf examined, even reached through a link: nothing is written beside the evidence; a directory within it, there already or to be made, since making one would change the evidence's directory; palimpsest's own directory in the cache, which is cleared; a directory where any of the names is there already. nothing is written over, nothing is copied, and the message says which are there. w writes one revision as a pdf to show to others, with its log beside it, and opens it. it asks which revision: the one whose part of the report the cursor is in, in a revision opened from the report that one, and elsewhere it asks revision to write (1-n) (n-1): , offering the one before the latest. then it asks where, offering name-revision-n.pdf in the directory last saved to, else your home directory. each copy's sum is checked and the message gives the sha-256. the pdf written is the file as it stood after that save, so palimpsest on it finds the saves up to it. modal non-modal command what it does none w , c-c p w palimpsest-write-revision write a revision of the pdf the report is on as a pdf of its own, to show to others, its log beside it: the revision whose part of the report point is in, or the revision shown, when this is a revision opened from the report; else asked for, the one before the latest offered. c-x c-w writes the report alone, as text. it offers name-revision-report.txt in the directory last saved to, else your home directory, and the message gives the sha-256. modal non-modal command what it does none c-x c-w palimpsest-write-report write the report as text to a file asked for, name-revision-report.txt offered in the directory last saved to, else in the home directory; never beside the pdf examined nor in the package's own directory, nothing written over. both refuse the same places as s : never beside the pdf examined, never in the cache directory, nothing written over. a directory named in *evidence-directories* is refused as well, saying which directory and which entry made it evidence. when you are asked where to save, give the case's own work directory, beside the evidence directory and not inside it. c-x k closes the report. when its copies were not saved it asks revisions and logs not saved: close and delete them? . y closes the report, deletes its directory and closes the revisions and logs opened from it. anything else keeps it. q in the report leaves its window as it was before and keeps the report, which c-x b goes back to; chapter 4 gives q its other use. try it, keeping a revision examine a pdf of your own that has more than one revision, with m-x palimpsest . the summary says how many. press c-s , type revision 1 and press ret to reach the original. press w and answer the two questions, giving a place of your own for the file. the earlier pdf opens. press c-x b and choose the report again. press tab to an object number under changes and press ret to see that object. press o and type a number to show another. press s and give a new directory. the echo row says how many files were saved. look in the directory for the .pdf , .log and report files. press c-x k . it closes at once, because the work was saved.","04-drawings":" drawings seeing where each object is on the page, and the shapes of a scan's characters. a finding that says \"text on page 1 is covered\" is easier to judge when you can see the page. d shows a copy of the pdf beside the report with each thing the page draws framed and named by the object that draws it. for a scanned page it also frames the places where signs were found in the pixels. t shows the shapes of a scan's characters grouped, a view to look at that makes no finding. this chapter covers both, how to keep what they show, and the colours and lines of the frames.","04-drawings#objects-framed-d":"4.1 objects framed: d d shows, in a window of its own down the right of the report, the pdf with each thing its pages draw framed and named by the object that draws it. q there closes it. d again, while it is shown, closes it too, and shows it again at once later. modal non-modal command what it does none d , c-c p d palimpsest-draw-objects show beside the report, in a window of its own that q there closes, a copy of the pdf with each thing its pages draw framed and named by the object that draws it: every line of text (each part of a line a content stream or form of its own draws, framed apart), every shape and picture, every annotation. text is no object of its own: a page's content stream draws it, a few operators to a line. so each stretch of a line that one stream or form draws, without a gap wider than one and a half times its size (a column apart), is framed on its own and named by that stream. the name gives where in its decoded octets the stretch's operators begin and the font objects it is set in. 4 @370 font 8 means content stream 4, from octet 370 of it, in font object 8. o and then 4 shows the stream. a retyped figure often stands apart there: at the end of the stream, or in a font of its own. the findings about runs of text and lines, such as another size, bolder, squeezed, spaced, another black, off the baseline or an edge, or a retyped figure, name the same place after each detail, (4 @370 font 8) . ret on 4 @370 shows object 4 with the cursor where those operators begin in its decoded stream. other things are framed this way: each shape over 4 points a side, dashed; each picture, with the number of its content stream, form or picture above its top left; each annotation, dashed, with its number and kind ( 15 freetext ). every content stream, form and picture has a colour of its own, so a stream that a later save appended stands out from the page's own. these colours are never red, blue or green. the copy shows the revision whose part of the report the cursor is in, else the latest. it is that revision octet for octet, and one save more that holds the drawing, the page's own drawing kept apart between q and q . it is written to the report's own directory, never beside the pdf examined, as objects-revision-n.pdf . where a font gives no widths, as the base fonts such as helvetica do not, the ends of its lines are reckoned and their frames may be a little wide. an encrypted pdf is not drawn, since a stream added to it would be read as encrypted too. the report stays where it is: read it while the page is beside it, and use o on a number to show that object. a key at the foot of the page says what each colour means. each line of the key begins with a piece of its frame, so it holds whatever the settings below are set to.","04-drawings#signs-in-a-scans-pixels":"4.2 signs in a scan's pixels on a scanned page d also frames what the checks of chapter 7 name in the pixels: words unlike the rest, copies, marks and words without grain of their own, pieces saved as jpegs, and characters not judged. each is framed by its strength: table 4.1. how a frame is drawn as standard. frame meaning red and whole a strong sign. blue and dashed a sign worth a look. dark green and dotted a character not judged. the colour of each is a setting, *palimpsest-strong-colour* , *palimpsest-look-colour* and *palimpsest-not-judged-colour* . a value is a colour as \"#rrggbb\" , a name such as :red , or a number of the 256 colours. how each is drawn is set by *palimpsest-strong-line* , *palimpsest-look-line* and *palimpsest-not-judged-line* , each :whole , :dashed or :dotted . setting default values what it changes *palimpsest-strong-colour* \"#e60000\" text, or a keyword, or a whole number from 0 to 255 the colour a strong sign is framed in on a scanned picture drawn by d. *palimpsest-look-colour* \"#1a4ce6\" text, or a keyword, or a whole number from 0 to 255 the colour a sign worth a look is framed in on a scanned picture drawn by d. *palimpsest-not-judged-colour* \"#007326\" text, or a keyword, or a whole number from 0 to 255 the colour a character not judged is framed in on a scanned picture drawn by d. setting default values what it changes *palimpsest-strong-line* :whole :whole, :dashed, :dotted how the frame of a strong sign is drawn on a scanned picture drawn by d. *palimpsest-look-line* :dashed :whole, :dashed, :dotted how the frame of a sign worth a look is drawn on a scanned picture drawn by d. *palimpsest-not-judged-line* :dotted :whole, :dashed, :dotted how the frame of a character not judged is drawn on a scanned picture drawn by d. a frame at the page's edge, on a scanned page of one picture, is drawn just inside it, with its label within the page.","04-drawings#a-picture-file":"4.3 a picture file d works on a picture file (png, jpeg or tiff) as it does on a scanned page of a pdf. the picture is examined as one scanned page, and d shows that page with the signs found on it drawn over it, each framed by its strength and labelled, the key at the foot of the page. ink the scanner clipped is hatched grey and characters not judged are framed in dark green. only the signs of the scan are drawn, because the page that stands for a picture holds the picture alone, with no text, shapes or annotations to frame. the drawing is written to the report's own directory, named after the picture: letter.png gives letter-drawing.pdf , never beside the picture examined. the picture is read again from its file to draw it, and nothing is drawn when the file has changed since it was examined.","04-drawings#the-shapes-of-the-characters-t":"4.4 the shapes of the characters: t when a scan shows no copies but something still feels off, t shows beside the report the shapes of the characters of the pdf's scanned pages. it is a view to look at, not a finding. palimpsest reads no text and claims nothing of what it shows: honest type differs too, in bold, italic, headings, figures, forms filled in and logos, and you look and decide. modal non-modal command what it does none t , c-c p t palimpsest-show-shapes show beside the report, in a window of its own that q there closes, the shapes of the characters of the pdf's scanned pages. each scanned page's characters are cut out as for the copies of chapter 7 and taken size by size. they are made black and white and scaled so that their size is 32 rows. two are grouped when, set together middle to middle or a pixel of the scan either way, both their weight and their form agree. the pixels inked in one of them only are under 0.3 of those inked in either, since a letter set bold has more. those more than a pixel of the scan from any ink of the other are under 0.025 of them, since a letter of another typeface, or in italic, has serifs, bowls or strokes elsewhere. each group is one row, with its count at the left and 32 characters at most, the characters least like the first one first. characters that match no other shape of their size on their page come last, each with its line and its place in it: 12:4 is line 12, the 4th character. every character drawn leads to its place on its scanned page. those pages follow the pages of shapes in the same pdf, with the characters that match nothing framed there with the same label. the view is the revision whose part of the report the cursor is in, else the latest, with one save more, made in the background and written to the report's own directory. the pdf itself is never changed. t again closes the view.","04-drawings#keeping-a-drawing":"4.5 keeping a drawing d writes the copy with its objects framed to a pdf of its own, of the revision the cursor is in, else the latest. it asks where, offering name-drawing.pdf in the directory last saved to, else your home directory. it never writes beside the pdf examined, writes nothing over a file, and makes the file read only. the document information of the file says what it is, naming the pdf, the revision and that each object it draws is framed, and that palimpsest made it. modal non-modal command what it does none d , c-c p d palimpsest-write-drawing write the copy with each object its pages draw framed and named (d) to a pdf of its own, to keep or show to others: of the revision whose part of the report point is in, else the latest. t does the same for the pages of shapes. modal non-modal command what it does none t , c-c p t palimpsest-write-shapes write the shapes of the characters of the pdf's scanned pages (t) to a pdf of its own, to keep or show to others: of the revision whose part of the report point is in, else the latest.","04-drawings#closing-a-drawing-q":"4.6 closing a drawing: q q in the report closes the copy with its objects drawn, or its pages of shapes, when one is shown. otherwise it gives the window back to what it showed before, and the report is kept: palimpsest on the same pdf shows it again at once, as c-x b does. modal non-modal command what it does none q , c-c p q palimpsest-quit in a report: close the copy with its objects drawn (d) or its pages of shapes (t) when it is shown; else put the window back as c-c q does, the report kept, shown again by m-x palimpsest on the same pdf. all these keys are also under the prefix c-c p : c-c p d , c-c p d , c-c p t , c-c p t and c-c p q . c-c names the prefix palimpsest . try it, drawing a page examine a pdf of your own with m-x palimpsest . press d . the page appears beside the report with frames and names. press tab to a finding that names a place like 4 @370 , and press ret to see the stream there. press d again to close the drawing. press it once more and it comes back at once. if the pdf has a scanned page, press t to see the shapes of its characters, then t again to close them. press d and give a directory of your own to keep the drawing.","05-many-files":" many files setting a directory of pdfs beside each other, and grouping files into cases. forgery rarely stands alone. the same signature picture turns up in several contracts, one stamp on many pages, one editor in a pile of invoices. two commands look across files. palimpsest-compare-files reads a directory of pdfs and finds what is the same in two of them. palimpsest-group-cases reads the fingerprints of reports you saved and groups the files that look made by one hand or one kit. this chapter covers both.","05-many-files#comparing-a-directory":"5.1 comparing a directory palimpsest-compare-files asks for a directory and sets beside one another the pdfs in it, not those in the directories under it, to find a signature, a stamp or a seal that is the same in two of them. pictures that are not scans are set beside one another by their octets. the marks of the scanned pages that are as large as a signature or a stamp are laid over one another pixel by pixel, as palimpsest does within one file. it lists the pictures that are in more than one file and the pairs of marks of two files that are the same. the message says how many pdfs, how many pictures in more than one and how many pairs of marks. it needs two pdfs or more, and reads at most 200 of them. the files are only read. the work runs in the background and takes as long as examining each of them takes. modal non-modal command what it does none m-x palimpsest-compare-files palimpsest-compare-files set beside one another the pdfs of a directory asked for, to find a signature, a stamp or a seal the same in two of them. no hand signs twice alike and no stamp presses twice alike. but an office's logo, or a signature it keeps as a picture, is honestly the same in every letter. whether the marks should differ is for you to judge.","05-many-files#cases":"5.2 cases every time you save a report with s , it writes name-fingerprint.txt beside the report. the fingerprint, described in chapter 2, holds the ways of editing found, the editing programs, how cross-references are kept and the jpeg tables of the file's pictures. it is plain text, with one kind: value on a line after palimpsest fingerprint 1 , the pdf's name and its sha-256. c in a report asks for a directory, reads every name-fingerprint.txt in it and in the directories under it, in the background, and shows *palimpsest: cases* . the command works anywhere with m-x as well. save the reports of the files of one matter, each to a directory under one directory, then use c on that directory. modal non-modal command what it does none c , c-c p c palimpsest-group-cases group the pdfs examined so far as cases. files are grouped as one case when they share a way of editing and one thing more (another way, a program a later save names, or the jpeg tables of a picture), and are alike by half or more. how alike two fingerprints are is the weight of what they share over the weight of what either has: table 5.1. what a fingerprint's parts weigh. shared weight a program a later save names 3 jpeg tables 2 a way of editing 1 how cross-references are kept 1/2 a program the original names 1/4 each case names what all its files share and how alike each two are. the files that are alike to no other are listed last, with how many ways of editing each shows. two files that only come from one source and were edited by no one are never a case. a group is a sign that one hand, or one kit, made the files: the same editor at the same quality and the same ways. it is for you to judge, with the rest of each case. it is not proof. try it, grouping reports examine two or three pdfs of your own, one after the other, with m-x palimpsest . in each report press s and give a directory of its own under one new directory, for example cases/one/ , cases/two/ . press c and give cases/ . *palimpsest: cases* lists the groups, and the files that stand alone last. run m-x palimpsest-compare-files and give a directory of pdfs to see the pictures and marks the same in more than one.","06-strengths":" strengths and how to read a finding what strong, worth a look and for the record mean, and how to weigh them. every finding has one of three strengths. the strength says how far an honest program could have left the same sign. it does not say how much was changed or how wrong it is. this chapter says what each strength means, how the strength is chosen, and how to read a finding with its honest causes before you decide.","06-strengths#the-three-strengths":"6.1 the three strengths table 6.1. the strengths. strength it means strong sign of editing what an honest program does not leave: a date before the file was made, a white box over text added later, a cross-reference section where none is said to be. worth a look what editing leaves, and some honest programs too: a later save, a pdf editor named, a font in two subsets. for the record what the file holds that an examiner will want to know: a signature, an embedded file, freed objects. in the report they are the headings ** strong signs of editing , ** worth a look and ** for the record . the first is the one to read first. a strong sign is a fact of the file that an honest program does not produce, not a judgment about the document. a strong sign can still have an honest meaning in your case: a stamp laid over a page in a later save is how a viewer signs a contract, and chapter 8 shows that. say what was found, and say why it is or is not what you expected.","06-strengths#how-the-strength-is-chosen":"6.2 how the strength is chosen the strength belongs to the rule that made the finding, sometimes raised by what was found. the same covering shape is a strong sign when it is white and worth a look in another colour. invisible text is for the record over a scanned page and strong when a later save added it. chapter 7 says, for every check, when its findings are which. how far a measure is off never sets the strength. the least amounts a rule names (30 grey levels of ink, 1.5 pixels, a tenth of a size) decide only whether a finding is made. a run of text in a size of its own is worth a look whether it is a tenth smaller or half, as a heading or a footnote is too. what raises a finding is a sign honest programs do not leave: pixels the same where no scanner makes them so, or a structure no program writes. a scanned or photographed word unlike its line is never strong, however many ways it is unlike it, for a real scan has such words too. when two measures apart from each other agree on one, so that it looks unlike its line and sits off it, the finding says so, and it stays worth a look. strong signs need the file to contradict itself: a startxref naming a section outside its save, a length that runs over a save of the file, a date before the file was made. a file that does not contradict itself gets, at most, a worth a look.","06-strengths#the-verdict-and-the-order":"6.3 the verdict and the order the verdict in the summary is the strongest strength found. the findings are listed strongest first, then by revision, then by page. each is put in the revision it is about, or, when it is about the file as a whole, in the latest.","06-strengths#reading-a-finding":"6.4 reading a finding read the sentence. it says what was found and what it means. read where: the revision, the page and the objects. ret on an object number shows it as that revision has it. d draws the page with the object framed. read the honest causes. a finding that can come from honest programs names them, for example heavy type in a scan, a stamp or a clipped scanner. open the place it points to and see whether one of them fits. open the revision before (chapter 3) and compare it with the last. the report sets the text of each changed page side by side. open the log for the finding to see the rule, the evidence and the steps to check it by hand. weigh what is outside the file: the other party's copy, the paper, how the file reached you.","06-strengths#checks-that-could-not-finish":"6.5 checks that could not finish in the log each finding names its check, and the * checks part of the report sums the findings up by check. a check that could not finish says so, for the record. treat that as a limit, not as a clean result: what the check would have read was not read. try it, reading a finding by its strength examine a pdf of your own that has a later save, with m-x palimpsest . in * findings , read the groups in order. note which finding sets the verdict. for one finding worth a look, read the honest causes in its sentence and open the object it names with ret . open the log of the revision it is in with ret on its name under * revisions , and read the rule and the steps to see it by hand . press q to close the log.","07-what-palimpsest-looks-for":" what palimpsest looks for every check, what it reads, and what honest files can show alike. palimpsest runs a number of checks on a file. each looks for one way of editing that an examiner knows by name, and each is a row of * checks in the report. the paragraph for each check below is the explanation that ret on its name in the report opens. it says how that editing is done, what it leaves, how the check finds it and what honest files can show alike. under it come the strengths its findings take (chapter 6) and the files it is run on. the checks run in the order of this chapter. each runs on its own: when one fails on a damaged file, the others still run. every check but those on pixels reads the pdf's objects, their dictionaries and the octets as written, and the operators in the content streams. \"ink\" there is a colour as an operator gives it, a number. some checks are not run when the file has nothing for them to read. the row in * checks says not run and why, and it says nothing either way about that kind of editing.","07-what-palimpsest-looks-for#how-the-file-is-put-together":"7.1 how the file is put together these checks read the structure of the file: its saves, its indexes, its objects and the octets between them. they run on every revision of a pdf and not on picture files.","07-what-palimpsest-looks-for#later-saves":"7.1.1 later saves a pdf can be changed without writing it anew: the program adds what it changes at the end of the file, a later save, and leaves the original before it. most editing of a finished pdf leaves such a save behind. the check counts the saves the file keeps and names each page a later save changes, so that the revisions can be set side by side. honest files have later saves too: a form filled in, a signature added, a reader's notes or a viewer that saves on closing all write one. strength: worth a look. runs on: a pdf.","07-what-palimpsest-looks-for#cross-reference-sections":"7.1.2 cross-reference sections each save ends in a cross-reference section, the file's index of where each object begins, and a trailer naming the section before it. editing a file by hand, or with a tool that patches it in place, leaves an index that no longer fits: entries pointing beside the object they name, a section naming no earlier one, a save indexed in another way than the first, or a file prepared for fast web viewing changed after. the check reads every section against the octets of the file. some honest programs leave empty slots in their index, and many store later saves in compressed object streams; those are named worth a look or for the record only. strength: strong for a startxref naming a section outside its save or none found, a save prepared for the web whose two parts disagree, and entries that do not point at the object they name. worth a look for other problems with the sections, a save indexed another way than the first and a linearised file changed later. for the record for hybrid files and for changes kept in object streams. runs on: a pdf.","07-what-palimpsest-looks-for#objects-freed-unused-or-written-twice":"7.1.3 objects freed, unused or written twice a pdf is made of numbered objects. removing or replacing something frees objects, or leaves them in the file with nothing using them; an edit done in haste writes an object twice in one save or reuses a number without raising its generation. the old objects keep what was there before. the check follows every reference from the document's root and lists what nothing reaches, what is freed and what is written twice. programs that save carelessly also leave unused objects, so these tell where to look rather than prove an edit. strength: worth a look for unused objects, objects no section lists, objects written twice and numbers used again without a higher generation. for the record for freed objects. runs on: a pdf.","07-what-palimpsest-looks-for#data-before-and-after-the-file":"7.1.4 data before and after the file a reader of a pdf looks for its first line and its last %%eof and skips anything outside them. data appended after the last save, or put before the file begins, is a place to hide a message or an earlier version. the check reads what lies beyond those ends and says what it is. a file sent by mail or copied from a disc can carry a few honest octets of padding there, which are named as such. strength: strong for 16 octets or more after the last %%eof that are not shaped as a printing system's banner or printer job lines, for pdf objects there, and for a kilobyte of white space. worth a look for a few octets, a banner, padding of one octet repeated, and octets before %pdf- . runs on: a pdf.","07-what-palimpsest-looks-for#white-space-hiding-data":"7.1.5 white space hiding data between a pdf's objects any amount of white space may stand, and a reader skips it. a program hiding a message can write it as a pattern of spaces, tabs and line ends there. the check looks for long runs of white space within the saves that mix their kinds as such a code writes them. writers pad with runs of one kind; a run of spaces between two line ends is not named. strength: strong for white space mixing its kinds, or a kilobyte of it, anywhere in the file outside the streams. worth a look for more white space than a line end. runs on: a pdf.","07-what-palimpsest-looks-for#saves-hidden-in-streams":"7.1.6 saves hidden in streams a stream holds a page's drawing, a picture or a font, and its stated length tells a reader where it ends. a save can be hidden inside a stream whose length is written to swallow it, or a stream left open so that a reader passes over the save that follows. the check sets each stream's stated length beside where it really ends, and looks for %%eof lines and object headers inside the data of streams that are not embedded files. a pdf kept inside another as an attachment holds those honestly, and is not named. strength: strong for a stream whose /length runs over a save of the file itself. worth a look for a %%eof within a stream whose end the file does not confirm, and for %%eof or object headers within the data of a stream that is no attached file. runs on: a pdf.","07-what-palimpsest-looks-for#comments-between-the-objects":"7.1.7 comments between the objects lines beginning with % between a pdf's objects are comments, which no reader shows. some editing tools write their name there, and some people hide notes in them. the check lists the comments and the programs they name. several honest writers leave a comment of their own as well. strength: worth a look for comments naming an editor. for the record otherwise. runs on: a pdf.","07-what-palimpsest-looks-for#what-the-file-says-of-itself":"7.2 what the file says of itself these read the file's own account of its making: its dates, its programs, and any credentials. they run on a picture file too.","07-what-palimpsest-looks-for#metadata-and-dates":"7.2.1 metadata and dates a file says of itself which program made it and when it was made and changed: in a pdf, its document information and its xmp metadata; in a picture, its exif, xmp and other fields. an editor writes its own name and a new date, or forgets to change one of two places that should agree. the check reads every field, sets the dates against each other and against today, and names programs that edit files. dates differ honestly by time zone, and a picture turned or cropped in a phone's gallery is saved again with a new date, so these are worth a look at most unless they cannot be true. strength: strong for a date last changed before it was made, a date in the future, and the document information and the xmp more than a day apart on a date. worth a look for other disagreements, odd time zones, edit histories naming an editor, a pdf editor named, another program in a later save, metadata removed and the first id changed. for the record for two time zones, dates compared only to the month or year, and the two parts of /id differing. runs on: a pdf or a picture file.","07-what-palimpsest-looks-for#content-credentials-and-generator-marks":"7.2.2 content credentials and generator marks some programs record how a picture or a document was made: content credentials (c2pa), the digital source type in xmp, and the marks image generators write. the check reads them, as data and nothing more, and names a picture an image generator says it made, or a later edit the credentials record. credentials can be stripped, so their absence says nothing. strength: worth a look, never strong, for a picture or pdf that says an image generator made it. for the record for content credentials that say nothing of a generator. runs on: a pdf or a picture file.","07-what-palimpsest-looks-for#what-the-pages-draw":"7.3 what the pages draw these read the instructions that draw each page, its fonts and its pictures, in the latest revision. the earlier pages are compared in the revisions' parts of the report.","07-what-palimpsest-looks-for#what-the-pages-draw-2":"7.3.1 what the pages draw a page is drawn by a list of instructions. editing text in a pdf often leaves the old text under a white box, makes it invisible, clips it away, moves it off the page, or adds new text drawn a character at a time in a font of its own. the check reads every page's instructions and looks for text covered, hidden or clipped, text appended later, figures placed unlike their font's widths, table cells set apart from their column, tagged text unlike what shows, and words a chatbot leaves in pasted text. some honest programs also draw a character at a time or leave text under shapes, so most of these are worth a look rather than strong. strength: strong for a white shape or shading over text, invisible or white text added in a later save, and a content stream appended in a later save. worth a look for covering shapes that are not white, text clipped, outside the page, in a layer that is off, white or tiny, lines drawn a character at a time or spaced apart, retyped figures, tags unlike the text, and text on a scan. for the record for ocr text over a scan and several content streams from one save. runs on: a pdf. not run when: no pages.","07-what-palimpsest-looks-for#fonts-and-widths":"7.3.2 fonts and widths text typed into a finished pdf has to use a font, and the editor often brings its own copy: a second subset of the same face, the face both embedded and not, a family named the way another program names it, or the same font with other widths for the same characters. the check sets the fonts of the pages against each other. a document joined from parts made by different programs shows some of the same, so these are worth a look at most. strength: strong for a font kept in the file that was last changed after the document was made. worth a look for a face in several subsets or embedded and not, and a font on some pages only. for the record for a font that is both simple and composite. runs on: a pdf. not run when: no text layer.","07-what-palimpsest-looks-for#text-that-copies-as-other-text":"7.3.3 text that copies as other text a font maps each code to the shape it draws and, apart from that, to the letter it stands for when text is copied or searched. a figure changed by editing the font's map rather than the page shows one number and copies as another. the check compares the two maps of every font a page draws with. old or badly made fonts can disagree honestly, so this is worth a look. strength: worth a look. runs on: a pdf. not run when: no text layer.","07-what-palimpsest-looks-for#pictures-unlike-the-others":"7.3.4 pictures unlike the others a picture pasted into a document, such as a signature or a logo, often differs from the rest in how it is stored, its colours or the program that made it, or was added in a later save. the check sets the pictures of the file against each other and reads what jpeg pictures say of the programs that made them. documents assembled honestly from many sources mix their pictures too. strength: worth a look for pictures unlike the others, of another resolution, added later, or keeping metadata that names a program. for the record for jpeg metadata. runs on: a pdf. not run when: no pictures.","07-what-palimpsest-looks-for#scanned-pages":"7.4 scanned pages a scanned page is a picture of paper. these checks read its pixels, once for all: the scan itself is decoded and no page is drawn as a picture to compare. a picture covering a third of a page or more is decoded to grey levels, made black and white at the level that best parts ink from paper, straightened, its ruled lines taken out and cut into lines, words and characters. the checks that follow set each word and character beside the others. a picture file is examined as one scanned page, so they run on it too. none of them runs when the file has no scanned page, and the row of * checks then says so.","07-what-palimpsest-looks-for#scanned-pages-read":"7.4.1 scanned pages read the checks of the scanned pages read their pixels. this row says what could not be read or judged, so that no page goes unexamined without its saying so: pictures in a form not read here or too large, pages whose characters are too alike or too few to tell prints from copies, bands of lines run together, characters with too little ink left once clipped ink is set aside. nothing here is a sign of editing; it bounds what the other rows can say. strength: for the record. it bounds what the other rows say. runs on: a pdf or a picture file. not run when: no scanned pages.","07-what-palimpsest-looks-for#pages-drawn-by-a-program":"7.4.2 pages drawn by a program a scanned page carries the grain of paper and a scanner's blur. a page made by opening a document in a picture editor, or by printing it as a picture, is drawn by a program from text and has neither. the check looks for whole pages of perfectly flat paper whose text edges are smoothed into half-tones. a screenshot, a page printed to a picture by an app, or a scan whose paper was whitened until it is one grey look the same, so this is worth a look. strength: worth a look, never strong. runs on: a pdf or a picture file. not run when: no scanned pages.","07-what-palimpsest-looks-for#words-unlike-their-neighbours":"7.4.3 words unlike their neighbours a word pasted or typed into a scanned page rarely matches the words around it: its ink is darker or lighter, its edges sharper or softer, its paper cleaner, or it sits off the line or turned against it. the check measures each word against those beside it and names those that stand apart, more so those apart both in how they look and in where they sit. real scans have uneven ink, stains and folds, so these are worth a look at most. strength: worth a look, never strong. runs on: a pdf or a picture file. not run when: no scanned pages.","07-what-palimpsest-looks-for#characters-copied-grain-and-all":"7.4.4 characters copied, grain and all to change a figure on a scanned page, an editor copies another character of the page over it, so that the ink and grain match. but a scanner never prints a character twice alike: each print differs in its grain and in where the blur falls on the pixels. the check sets every character beside the others of its shape and names those that share a block of pixels the same, grey level for grey level, with the grain of a print, and those copied and then saved again as a jpeg, which stay nearer each other than prints come. a stamp or a signature laid on each page by software is the same every time honestly, and clean prints on a page kept as a jpeg can come near each other by chance; those are named worth a look. strength: strong for characters the same pixel for pixel, the grain of their ink too, on a page whose grey levels are not stepped alike. worth a look for characters nearer one another than prints are, however near, and for the honest causes named in the finding. runs on: a pdf or a picture file. not run when: no scanned pages.","07-what-palimpsest-looks-for#characters-the-same-pixel-for-pixel":"7.4.5 characters the same pixel for pixel characters drawn into a picture by a program, as a picture editor's text tool or an office's stamp software draws them, come out the same pixel for pixel every time, and marks so drawn have no grain of their own. the check names characters that repeat exactly where a program could have drawn them: in an ink or at an angle unlike the page's text, far lighter than it, or on a page whose prints come out alike (a jpeg kept without loss, few grey levels, black and white smoothed, faint grain); and marks without grain that repeat exactly. honest stamps, light printed forms and a scanner that clips dark ink to one grey make prints alike in the same way, so this is worth a look. strength: worth a look. runs on: a pdf or a picture file. not run when: no scanned pages.","07-what-palimpsest-looks-for#text-typed-into-the-pixels":"7.4.6 text typed into the pixels text typed onto a scanned page with a picture editor's text tool is drawn by the program in one flat ink, while every character a scanner prints strays by some grey levels however wide its strokes. the check names words and lines whose characters have no grain of their own. bold or heavier type, a stamp, coloured ink or a scanner that clips dark ink can look the same, so this is worth a look. strength: worth a look. runs on: a pdf or a picture file. not run when: no scanned pages.","07-what-palimpsest-looks-for#text-layer-against-the-pixels":"7.4.7 text layer against the pixels a scanned pdf often carries an invisible text layer, made by reading the page, so that it can be searched. when the picture is edited and the layer is not, the two disagree: the layer holds words over blank paper, or the picture holds words the layer lacks. the check sets each page's layer against its pixels. a layer read badly by the scanner's software disagrees honestly, so this is worth a look at most. strength: worth a look. for the record for ocr text over a scan. runs on: a pdf. not run when: no scanned pages or no text layer.","07-what-palimpsest-looks-for#pieces-off-the-jpeg-grid":"7.4.8 pieces off the jpeg grid a jpeg stores a picture in blocks of 8 by 8 pixels on a grid. a piece cut from one picture, saved as a jpeg of its own and laid into another keeps its own blocks, off the page's grid; a piece laid in after the page was saved lacks the blocks the rest carries. the check measures each region's blocks against the page's grid. a region is strong only when another sign agrees on it, since honest software that lays a logo or a stamp on a scan leaves the same. strength: strong for a region carrying jpeg blocks of its own when another sign agrees on it. worth a look otherwise. runs on: a pdf or a picture file. not run when: no scanned pages.","07-what-palimpsest-looks-for#regions-without-the-jpegs-traces":"7.4.9 regions without the jpeg's traces every part of a page saved as a jpeg carries the traces of that save. paint laid over the page afterwards, or a region pasted from a picture never saved so, lacks them; a page joined from strips can have one strip saved with other tables than the rest. the check measures the traces tile by tile and names regions that lack them or were painted flat. very smooth paper, or a scanner that sets its quality strip by strip, can look the same, so these are worth a look at most. strength: worth a look. runs on: a pdf or a picture file. not run when: no scanned pages.","07-what-palimpsest-looks-for#paper-copied-over-paper":"7.4.10 paper copied over paper a clone stamp or a content-aware fill removes a word by copying paper from elsewhere on the page over it. the paper brings its grain along, and no two patches of a scanner's paper are ever alike pixel for pixel. the check searches pictures kept without loss for patches of paper near the words that match other paper of the page exactly at one shift. a form's printed pattern repeating at one shift matches too, so this is worth a look. strength: worth a look. runs on: a pdf or a picture file. not run when: no scanned pages.","07-what-palimpsest-looks-for#an-image-generators-pattern":"7.4.11 an image generator's pattern an image generator draws its picture from one eight times smaller, and its decoder leaves a faint pattern repeating every 8 by 8 pixels that changes along rows and columns at once. a scanner, a camera and a jpeg leave none such. the check measures that pattern in the flat parts of pictures kept without loss. a picture tiled with a faint pattern of that size, or a jpeg saved twice on grids out of step, carries one too, so this is worth a look. strength: worth a look, never strong. runs on: a pdf or a picture file. not run when: no scanned pages.","07-what-palimpsest-looks-for#pictures-and-marks-repeated":"7.4.12 pictures and marks repeated no hand signs twice alike and no stamp presses twice alike. a signature or a stamp copied from one place to another is the same in both. the check lists pictures drawn in more than one place and lays the large marks of the scanned pages, those the size of a signature or a stamp, over each other pixel by pixel. offices lay the same stamp or logo on their letters honestly, so whether the marks should differ is for the examiner to judge. strength: worth a look and for the record. whether the marks should differ is for you to judge. runs on: a pdf or a picture file. not run when: no pictures.","07-what-palimpsest-looks-for#scan-resolution":"7.4.13 scan resolution how sharp the edges of the ink are tells the resolution a page was really scanned at, whatever its file says. a page made larger, or pieced together from scans of different resolutions, shows edges softer than its pixels. the check measures the edges of each scanned page and names the resolution, or says it was made larger. a page blurred, out of focus or printed soft cannot be told from one made larger, so this is for the record. strength: for the record. runs on: a pdf or a picture file. not run when: no scanned pages.","07-what-palimpsest-looks-for#pages-kept-as-jbig2-symbols":"7.4.14 pages kept as jbig2 symbols jbig2 can store a black and white page as a dictionary of character shapes, drawing each character from the shape it is nearest. such a scanner can put one figure in the place of another, and makes prints of a character the same everywhere, which the copy checks then cannot judge. the check reads only the segment headers and names the pages so kept. this is for the record. strength: for the record. runs on: a pdf. not run when: no scanned pages. a scanned word that is unlike its line is never strong: a real scan has such words too. characters that are copies of one another to the grain of their ink are the strongest sign a scan gives, since no scanner prints a character twice alike. a page printed and scanned again defeats all these signs, because the printer and the scanner lay new ink, grain, edges and jpeg blocks over the whole page, the forged parts and the rest alike. what the new file says of itself and what its words say is then all that is left to judge. d frames what these checks find in the pixels (chapter 4), and t shows the shapes of the characters beside the report.","07-what-palimpsest-looks-for#picture-files":"7.5 picture files these two read the jpeg pictures themselves and run on a picture file, with the second also on the jpeg pictures of a pdf.","07-what-palimpsest-looks-for#a-jpeg-saved-twice":"7.5.1 a jpeg saved twice a jpeg opened in a picture editor and saved again is rounded a second time with other steps, and its frequencies no longer fall evenly over the steps of its last save. the check measures how far they stray and names the tables the jpeg uses, those of a common library at a quality or a device's own. a scanner or phone app that saves twice, a viewer that turned the picture, or an honest crop does the same, so this is worth a look at most. strength: worth a look. runs on: a picture file. not run when: not a jpeg.","07-what-palimpsest-looks-for#jpeg-headers-and-thumbnails":"7.5.2 jpeg headers and thumbnails a camera or scanner writes its own make of jpeg header and a small thumbnail of the picture in its exif. a picture editor writes another header, and an edit made after the thumbnail leaves the thumbnail showing the picture as it was. the check sets the header beside the device the exif names and the thumbnail beside the picture. a picture turned with its old thumbnail kept, or a device unknown here, can differ honestly. strength: worth a look. for the record for jpeg metadata. runs on: a pdf or a picture file. not run when: not a jpeg or no jpeg pictures.","07-what-palimpsest-looks-for#what-is-laid-over-a-page-and-what-the-file-allows":"7.6 what is laid over a page, and what the file allows these read what a later save can add on top of a finished page and what the file permits.","07-what-palimpsest-looks-for#annotations":"7.6.1 annotations annotations are laid over a page rather than drawn in it: notes, stamps, form widgets and boxes. an annotation that looks like the page, a white box over a figure with a new figure on it, can change what a reader sees without touching the page. the check names annotations that look like the page, were added later, are hidden, or are marked for redaction and never applied. honest reviewers add notes and stamps too. strength: strong for a freetext, stamp, ink, square or redact annotation added in a later save. worth a look for annotations that pass for the page, hidden annotations and redactions not applied. for the record for annotations there from the start. runs on: a pdf.","07-what-palimpsest-looks-for#form-fields":"7.6.2 form fields a form's fields hold values apart from the page. the check names fields whose values change from one save to the next, and xfa forms, which can show other content than the pdf's own pages. filling a form in honestly changes its values too; the saves show when and what. strength: worth a look. runs on: a pdf.","07-what-palimpsest-looks-for#layers-hidden-as-the-file-opens":"7.6.3 layers hidden as the file opens a pdf can hold layers that are off when it opens, so their content is in the file but not shown. text or a picture can be kept there unseen, or an old version left under a new one. the check names the layers off as the file opens. drawings and maps hide layers honestly. strength: worth a look. runs on: a pdf.","07-what-palimpsest-looks-for#signatures":"7.6.4 signatures a digital signature covers the octets of the file up to its save; what later saves change is outside it. the check reads what each signature claims, whether its octets cover its whole save, what later saves change, and objects hidden in the signed part and brought into use later. signing more than once, or filling in a form after signing, adds later saves honestly. strength: strong for a /byterange that is unreadable, leaves out more than the signature's value, stops short or covers another save, for a later save that changes what is shown or makes changes its permissions do not allow, and for a shadow attack. worth a look for changes after a signature that its permissions allow. for the record for the signatures themselves. runs on: a pdf.","07-what-palimpsest-looks-for#pages-added-removed-or-moved":"7.6.5 pages added, removed or moved pages can be put in, taken out or put in another order in a later save, and page labels changed to hide it. a page brought in from another document often differs in size or make from the rest. the check sets each save's pages against the save before, and the pages against each other. documents joined honestly from several files mix their pages too. strength: worth a look. runs on: a pdf.","07-what-palimpsest-looks-for#actions-and-embedded-files":"7.6.6 actions and embedded files a pdf can carry javascript, actions run on opening, programs to launch, addresses to visit and files kept inside it. none of these is run here; they are named. they can change what a reader sees or hide a document inside the document. many honest forms carry scripts and links. strength: worth a look for actions that run code or send data and for embedded files added later. for the record for actions that go to places and for embedded files. runs on: a pdf.","07-what-palimpsest-looks-for#encryption":"7.6.7 encryption encryption limits who can open a pdf and what they may do with it. encryption added in a later save, or permissions changed, tell of a file handled after it was made. the check names the encryption of each save and its permissions. many honest files are encrypted against printing or copying from the start. strength: worth a look for encryption added later. for the record for encryption from the start. runs on: a pdf.","07-what-palimpsest-looks-for#what-palimpsest-cannot-prove":"7.7 what palimpsest cannot prove these are the limits of what the checks can say. an earlier save that was erased without trace cannot be found. a file written again whole, with nothing of the earlier text left in it, has no earlier save to read. a save hidden inside a stream is inferred from the octets up to a %%eof that lies in the data. it is worth a look and never more, since nothing in the file names that save. attached files are taken at their word only when the document reaches them, their data begins with %pdf- and their own sections list their own objects. any other stream that holds %%eof or object headers gets a worth a look. a file attached by a tool that links nothing is such a stream. pages are compared by their text and drawing, as the operators say, and not by pixels. streams that honestly quote pdf syntax, such as xmp, scripts and fonts, can give a worth a look. only the first 5 suspicious streams get a comparison of the pages they would make. when both readings of the last startxref fit, counted from the first octet and from %pdf- , the first octet, base 0, is taken. chapter 9 lists what palimpsest does not read at all. try it, reading what a check looks for examine a pdf of your own with m-x palimpsest . in * checks , press tab to the row of a check and press ret . the help buffer shows the paragraph of that check from this chapter. press q to close it. find a row that says not run and see which reason it gives. look in this chapter for the check to see which files it is run on. find a row with a finding and press tab to the finding in * findings . compare its honest causes with the check's paragraph here.","08-signed-contracts":" examining examples: signed contracts how a contract comes to be a pdf, and what palimpsest says of each way. a contract reaches you as a pdf and the question is whether it is what the parties signed. each way of making and signing a contract leaves a file of its own shape, and most of what palimpsest says only makes sense against that shape. this chapter describes the two shapes of pdf, the common ways of making and signing a contract, what palimpsest says of each, and what it cannot see. the findings below are shown in a short form, as strong, look and record for the three strengths of chapter 6, with the page.","08-signed-contracts#what-a-pdf-holds-in-two-shapes":"8.1 what a pdf holds, in two shapes a page made by a program (a word processor, a drawing program, a pdf printer). the pdf keeps the text as text: the page's drawing holds runs of characters with their font and place, and the fonts are kept in the file. there is no object for each paragraph or section. text boxes and lines become runs inside the one drawing of the page. a scanned page . the pdf keeps a picture of the paper for each page, and a drawing of one line that puts it over the whole page. everything on the paper, printed text, handwriting and stamps, is pixels in that picture. there are no fonts, unless the scanner read the text (ocr) and laid an invisible text layer over the picture. an office machine saving a compact pdf splits each page into layers: a background picture, often a jpeg, and a black and white mask that holds all the dark ink, kept as jbig2 or ccitt. the signature is then in that mask, with the printed text, and not apart from it. so a pen signature is never an object of its own in an honest scan. a signature that is a picture of its own, laid on a page, was put there by a computer. poppler's pdfimages -list file lists the pictures of each page, their size and how they are stored, pdffonts file the fonts, and pdfinfo file the creator and producer. palimpsest reads all of this itself. these are quick checks by hand.","08-signed-contracts#the-common-ways-a-contract-is-made-and-signed":"8.2 the common ways a contract is made and signed table 8.1. how a contract is made, and what its file holds. how it was done made by pages hold written in a program, the program and text and fonts, pictures only exported to pdf, not its pdf writer for logos. one save. signed printed, signed with a the machine or one picture per page, or the pen, scanned on an its software compact layers. one save. office machine printed, signed, scanned the app one picture per page, the with a phone app paper's edges and light uneven, often turned or straightened. one save. scanned, then joined, the drawing the scanner's pictures, often turned or cropped in a program or the saved again. one save or drawing program or pdf pdf editor more. editor a signature picture the program and text and fonts, and a small pasted in, then exported its pdf writer picture where the signature is. one save. signed in a pdf viewer the first as before, and a signature program's, as added as an annotation or new before drawing. two saves or more. signed through a signing the service text, a signature picture or service typed name, often a page of the audit trail, and a cryptographic signature. one save or more. three things follow from the table. nothing survives paper. a contract written in a drawing program, printed, signed and scanned carries the scanner's name and not the program's. the drawing program as the creator of a scanned contract means someone opened the scan in it and exported it again, after it was scanned. one save proves little. every program that writes a file anew writes one save. exporting again throws away any history the scan had. a later save is where a viewer's signature goes. signing in a viewer keeps the old file and adds the change after it (chapter 3).","08-signed-contracts#what-palimpsest-says-of-each":"8.3 what palimpsest says of each","08-signed-contracts#an-honest-scan":"8.3.1 an honest scan a scan of a signed contract from an office machine names the machine as the producer and has no creator, one picture to a page and no fonts. palimpsest says, for example: look p1 on page 1, a line of the scanned page has no grain of its own: drawn into the scan by a program, or in bold or heavier type, or ink, a stamp or coloured ink the scanner clipped. record the ink's edges of the scanned pictures on pages 1 and 2 are as sharp as a scan's at about 300 dpi. the worth-a-look is a title set in bold: heavy type holds its ink solid, with less of the scanner's grain than the body text. the finding names that honest cause itself. open the page and look at the line it points to with d : a title, a bold heading or a dark stamp settles it, and an ordinary line of body text would not. the record line says the scan is a real scan at the resolution the file claims. this is what an honest scan looks like to palimpsest: nothing strong, perhaps a look at heavy ink, and the resolution for the record.","08-signed-contracts#a-file-written-in-one-go":"8.3.2 a file written in one go a contract written in a program and exported once, never changed, gets no finding. it is what it says it is, and is the shape to compare the others with.","08-signed-contracts#a-scan-saved-again-in-a-drawing-program":"8.3.3 a scan saved again in a drawing program someone opened the scan to join, turn or change it and exported it. the creator is the drawing program, and the pages are still one picture each with no fonts and one save. palimpsest says what it said of the scan and may add: look p1 2 characters of the scanned page are nearer one another than prints of one character are: copied within the scan and saved again, or alike by chance, as clean prints on pages kept as jpeg or jpeg 2000, or smoothed, can. the file's history is gone, so no save, layer or object tells what was done. what is left to judge is the pictures. two characters alike after a second jpeg save happen. a word or a figure alike pixel for pixel would not. palimpsest does not count a drawing program as the creator of a scanned page as a sign. you should: ask who opened it and why.","08-signed-contracts#a-signature-picture-pasted-into-a-page-made-by-a-program":"8.3.4 a signature picture pasted into a page made by a program a document made by a program may honestly carry a picture of a signature, and with one picture there is nothing to set it against, for the pictures check compares the pictures of a file with each other. palimpsest finds nothing. it does not say \"a signature on a page made by a program\". you see it in the structure: pdfimages -list shows one small picture on a page of text, at the signature line. if the contract was said to be signed by hand on paper, this file was not.","08-signed-contracts#signed-later-in-a-pdf-viewer":"8.3.5 signed later in a pdf viewer the signature is a picture with its transparency in a stamp annotation, added in a second save: strong p2 a stamp annotation (object 82) lies on page 2, added in revision 2 (the current file): it can look like part of the page, but is laid over it. look the file was changed after it was first saved: 1 later save is kept in it, beside what it changed. the strong finding is a fact of the file and not a judgment: the signature is laid over the page, in a later save. that is exactly what signing in a viewer does, so it is honest when that is how the contract was meant to be signed. open revision 1 to see the contract as it was before, unsigned. when the signature was said to be written on paper, it was not.","08-signed-contracts#a-page-swapped":"8.3.6 a page swapped a page printed again with other terms, scanned in another run and put before the signed page of the first scan, by the same machine, gets no finding beyond those of the honest scan. this is a known miss. two scans by one machine at one setting look alike to every check, and pages swapped before the pdf was written leave no save behind. what can show it is outside the file: the copy each party kept, the page against a draft or an e-mailed version, the paper. inside the file, look at each page's angle and paper (the record lines name the resolution of each page) and read the terms against the signed page. initials on every page are why contracts ask for them.","08-signed-contracts#signing-services":"8.4 signing services a contract signed through a signing service or a bank's identity service carries a cryptographic signature: a /sig field whose octets cover the file as it was signed. palimpsest reads what each signature claims, whether it covers its whole save, and what any later save changed (chapter 7). signing twice, or a service adding its audit trail, adds later saves honestly. a change to the pages after the last signature does not. palimpsest does not verify the certificate. acrobat or the service's own check does.","08-signed-contracts#a-checklist":"8.5 a checklist before palimpsest, ask how the contract was said to be made and signed, and find its row in table 8.1. run pdfinfo . do the creator and producer fit that way? a scan should name a scanner or a scanning app. a drawing program or word processor on a scan means it was opened and exported again. run pdfimages -list . one picture per page means a scan. a small picture on a page of text is a pasted signature. run palimpsest. read the strong signs first: they are facts of the file, such as an annotation laid over a page or a page changed in a later save. read each finding worth a look with its honest causes, and open the place it names before deciding. open each earlier revision and compare it with the last. remember what palimpsest cannot see: pages swapped before the pdf was written and a pasted signature on a page made by a program. compare with the other party's copy when it matters. try it, checking a contract open a signed pdf of your own, and run pdfinfo and pdfimages -list on it in a terminal. decide which row of table 8.1 it fits. examine it with m-x palimpsest and compare the verdict with what you expected from that row. if it has a later save, open revision 1 with w and see whether it is the contract before it was signed. press d and look at the place each finding names.","09-settings-and-limits":" settings and limits how much of a file palimpsest reads, and what it leaves unread. palimpsest reads files from anywhere, some made to do harm. everything it reads is held to a size, so that no file can make it run without end or fill the memory. what it leaves unread, the report says in not read in the summary (chapter 2). this chapter lists the settings that set the sizes, the limits that are fixed, and what palimpsest does not do.","09-settings-and-limits#the-settings":"9.1 the settings set them in the package's :config in your init file: (load-package \"palimpsest\" :source \"https://github.com/tuatara-editor/tuatara-palimpsest\" :config (setf *palimpsest-most-decoded* (* 16 1024 1024 1024))) c-h v on a setting shows its help once the package is loaded. a size is a whole number of octets, or words such as 512m , 1.5g or 20 gb , in units of setting default values what it changes *palimpsest-largest-file* 536870912 a whole number of octets, 1 or more, or a size in words such as 512m, 1.5g or 20 gb (in 1024s: 1k is 1024 octets), at most 4e the most octets a pdf may have to be examined. *palimpsest-largest-stream* 67108864 a whole number of octets, 1 or more, or a size in words such as 512m, 1.5g or 20 gb (in 1024s: 1k is 1024 octets), at most 4e the most octets one stream of a pdf may decode to, and a page's content streams together. *palimpsest-most-decoded* 8589934592 a whole number of octets, 1 or more, or a size in words such as 512m, 1.5g or 20 gb (in 1024s: 1k is 1024 octets), at most 4e the most octets of streams decoded and of page content read while one pdf is examined, all together. *palimpsest-most-objects* 1000000 a whole number, 1 or more the most objects of a pdf read, and the most entries one cross-reference section may list. *palimpsest-most-revisions* 500 a whole number, 1 or more the most saves (revisions) of a pdf taken apart. *palimpsest-largest-written* 2147483648 a whole number of octets, 1 or more, or a size in words such as 512m, 1.5g or 20 gb (in 1024s: 1k is 1024 octets), at most 4e the most octets the revisions of one pdf, each written out as a pdf of its own, may take together. *palimpsest-largest-shown* 1048576 a whole number of octets, 1 or more, or a size in words such as 512m, 1.5g or 20 gb (in 1024s: 1k is 1024 octets), at most 4e the most octets of an object's stream shown by o. *palimpsest-scan-pixels* 60000000 a whole number, 1 or more the most pixels a picture may have for its words to be measured. a pdf larger than *palimpsest-largest-file* is not examined; it is read whole, into memory, once. a stream that would decode to more than *palimpsest-largest-stream* is read no further, and the same holds for a page's content streams together. past *palimpsest-most-decoded* nothing more is decoded, a stream decoded again counting again. the revisions written out may take *palimpsest-largest-written* together. past it, or when the drive would keep less than 1 gb free, a revision is listed as not written. *palimpsest-largest-shown* bounds what o shows of a stream. *palimpsest-scan-pixels* bounds the scanned pictures whose words are measured, and a larger one is passed over. the settings for the colours and lines of the drawing are in chapter 4.","09-settings-and-limits#fixed-limits":"9.2 fixed limits these are not settings: at most 5,000,000 cross-reference entries in all the sections of one file, and 1,000,000 from one section; references followed 64 deep, the page tree read 64 deep, a node met twice passed over; a form's fields read 32 deep and 10,000 at most; forms drawn within forms followed 8 deep; at most 2,000,000 operators of one page, and 50,000,000 in all the pages of a file, every save's pages together; a font's tounicode map read for 131,072 codes at most, from a stream of 4 mb at most; the xmp metadata from a stream of 16 mb at most; a cross-reference section not where startxref says is looked for up to 64 octets either side; at most 64 cross-reference sections followed by /prev ; at most 100,000 %%eof lines outside streams read, 500 saves taken apart ( *palimpsest-most-revisions* ), and 5 saves inferred within streams compared with the save holding them. past a bound the report says so, under not read .","09-settings-and-limits#what-palimpsest-does-not-do":"9.3 what palimpsest does not do encrypted files are not decrypted, so their pages' content, fonts and pictures are not read. how the file is put together, its saves and its cross-reference sections are. signatures are not verified. no certificate or digest is checked. the report gives what a signature claims and which octets it covers. the signer's name is read from the signature's own data, as data. pictures are decoded for the scanned pages alone : a picture covering a third of a page or more. a jpeg or a jpeg 2000 is turned into pixels in the sandbox. a picture kept as fax or jbig2 is drawn by poppler's pdftoppm in the sandbox, and one that cannot be read is named in the report. other pictures are read only as their dictionaries say, and for a jpeg the metadata in its own segments. text in type 3 fonts with no tounicode map, and text drawn as pictures or shapes, is not read as text. text drawn as shapes is not measured as ink either: only a scanned picture's pixels are. it shows as shapes the page draws. positions follow the operators : transformations, text matrices and spacing, and the widths each font gives. clipping is followed as rectangles, the box around each clipping path. what pages draw is checked in the latest revision. the earlier revisions' pages are compared, each with the one before. a field lock is not read on its own. a certification's permissions are. chapter 7 ends with what the checks cannot prove. try it, changing a limit add a line to the :config of the load-package line in your init file that sets *palimpsest-most-revisions* to 2. restart tuatara and examine a pdf with more than two saves. read not read in the summary. it says that only the first saves were taken apart. take the line out again and restart.","10-every-command-and-setting":" every command and setting each command and setting of palimpsest, to look one up by name. the tables are made from the program each time this manual is made, so they never list a command or setting that palimpsest does not have. the first table has four columns. the first two are keys: the modal keys, which work only with modal editing on, and the non-modal keys, which work with it off too. a command with no key says m-x and its name in the second column, because you can only run it by its name. the third column is the name of the command and the fourth says what it does. to run a command by its name, press m-x , type a few letters of the name and press ret . to find a command from within the editor, press c-h f . to find the command that a key runs, press c-h k and then the key. the editor's own settings and commands are in chapters 21 and 22 of its manual.","10-every-command-and-setting#every-command":"10.1 every command modal non-modal command what it does none m-x palimpsest palimpsest examine a pdf, or a picture (png, jpeg or tiff), for signs of editing, from its octets alone. none m-x palimpsest-compare-files palimpsest-compare-files set beside one another the pdfs of a directory asked for, to find a signature, a stamp or a seal the same in two of them. none d , c-c p d palimpsest-draw-objects show beside the report, in a window of its own that q there closes, a copy of the pdf with each thing its pages draw framed and named by the object that draws it: every line of text (each part of a line a content stream or form of its own draws, framed apart), every shape and picture, every annotation. none c , c-c p c palimpsest-group-cases group the pdfs examined so far as cases. none q , c-c p q palimpsest-quit in a report: close the copy with its objects drawn (d) or its pages of shapes (t) when it is shown; else put the window back as c-c q does, the report kept, shown again by m-x palimpsest on the same pdf. none s , c-c p s palimpsest-save save the revisions of the pdf the report is on, each with its log, and the report itself as name-revision-report.txt, to a directory asked for (made when it is not there): the work to keep, as the report's own copies go when it is closed. none o , c-c p o palimpsest-show-object show an object of the pdf the report is on, read only: its place in the file, its dictionary as written and its stream decoded (at most palimpsest-largest-shown ), as text, or in hex in a buffer of its own when it is not text or with c-u. none t , c-c p t palimpsest-show-shapes show beside the report, in a window of its own that q there closes, the shapes of the characters of the pdf's scanned pages. none d , c-c p d palimpsest-write-drawing write the copy with each object its pages draw framed and named (d) to a pdf of its own, to keep or show to others: of the revision whose part of the report point is in, else the latest. none c-x c-w palimpsest-write-report write the report as text to a file asked for, name-revision-report.txt offered in the directory last saved to, else in the home directory; never beside the pdf examined nor in the package's own directory, nothing written over. none w , c-c p w palimpsest-write-revision write a revision of the pdf the report is on as a pdf of its own, to show to others, its log beside it: the revision whose part of the report point is in, or the revision shown, when this is a revision opened from the report; else asked for, the one before the latest offered. none t , c-c p t palimpsest-write-shapes write the shapes of the characters of the pdf's scanned pages (t) to a pdf of its own, to keep or show to others: of the revision whose part of the report point is in, else the latest.","10-every-command-and-setting#every-setting":"10.2 every setting setting default values what it changes *palimpsest-largest-file* 536870912 a whole number of octets, 1 or more, or a size in words such as 512m, 1.5g or 20 gb (in 1024s: 1k is 1024 octets), at most 4e the most octets a pdf may have to be examined. *palimpsest-largest-shown* 1048576 a whole number of octets, 1 or more, or a size in words such as 512m, 1.5g or 20 gb (in 1024s: 1k is 1024 octets), at most 4e the most octets of an object's stream shown by o. *palimpsest-largest-stream* 67108864 a whole number of octets, 1 or more, or a size in words such as 512m, 1.5g or 20 gb (in 1024s: 1k is 1024 octets), at most 4e the most octets one stream of a pdf may decode to, and a page's content streams together. *palimpsest-largest-written* 2147483648 a whole number of octets, 1 or more, or a size in words such as 512m, 1.5g or 20 gb (in 1024s: 1k is 1024 octets), at most 4e the most octets the revisions of one pdf, each written out as a pdf of its own, may take together. *palimpsest-look-colour* \"#1a4ce6\" text, or a keyword, or a whole number from 0 to 255 the colour a sign worth a look is framed in on a scanned picture drawn by d. *palimpsest-look-line* :dashed :whole, :dashed, :dotted how the frame of a sign worth a look is drawn on a scanned picture drawn by d. *palimpsest-most-decoded* 8589934592 a whole number of octets, 1 or more, or a size in words such as 512m, 1.5g or 20 gb (in 1024s: 1k is 1024 octets), at most 4e the most octets of streams decoded and of page content read while one pdf is examined, all together. *palimpsest-most-objects* 1000000 a whole number, 1 or more the most objects of a pdf read, and the most entries one cross-reference section may list. *palimpsest-most-revisions* 500 a whole number, 1 or more the most saves (revisions) of a pdf taken apart. *palimpsest-not-judged-colour* \"#007326\" text, or a keyword, or a whole number from 0 to 255 the colour a character not judged is framed in on a scanned picture drawn by d. *palimpsest-not-judged-line* :dotted :whole, :dashed, :dotted how the frame of a character not judged is drawn on a scanned picture drawn by d. *palimpsest-scan-pixels* 60000000 a whole number, 1 or more the most pixels a picture may have for its words to be measured. *palimpsest-strong-colour* \"#e60000\" text, or a keyword, or a whole number from 0 to 255 the colour a strong sign is framed in on a scanned picture drawn by d. *palimpsest-strong-line* :whole :whole, :dashed, :dotted how the frame of a strong sign is drawn on a scanned picture drawn by d."};
// The manual's pages: filter the contents column to the headings whose title or
// text holds what is typed in its box, and change between light and dark
// colours (the choice kept in this browser for the next page). The entry for
// the part being read is marked as the page scrolls. The text of
// every section comes before this script, in window.tuataraManualText, made
// when the manual is built. Links are left alone. No address is ever loaded.
(function () {
  var root = document.documentElement;
  var key = "tuatara-manual-theme";
  var saved = null;
  try { saved = window.localStorage.getItem(key); } catch (e) { saved = null; }
  if (saved === "light" || saved === "dark") { root.setAttribute("data-theme", saved); }

  function dark() {
    var chosen = root.getAttribute("data-theme");
    if (chosen) { return chosen === "dark"; }
    return Boolean(window.matchMedia && window.matchMedia("(prefers-color-scheme: dark)").matches);
  }

  var button = document.querySelector("button.theme");
  if (button) {
    var label = function () {
      button.textContent = dark() ? "Light" : "Dark";
      button.setAttribute("aria-label", dark() ? "Use light colours" : "Use dark colours");
    };
    button.hidden = false;
    label();
    button.addEventListener("click", function () {
      var next = dark() ? "light" : "dark";
      root.setAttribute("data-theme", next);
      try { window.localStorage.setItem(key, next); } catch (e) { /* kept for this page only */ }
      label();
    });
  }

  var nav = document.querySelector("nav.toc");
  var box = nav && nav.querySelector("input");
  if (!box) { return; }
  var texts = window.tuataraManualText || {};

  // The key of a section in the text: a chapter's page name without its
  // ending, then # and the heading's id. On the page of every chapter the
  // links are "10-directories.html#marks", on the whole manual "#10-directories--marks".
  function keyOf(href) {
    var parts = /^#?([0-9]+-[^#.]*?)(?:\.html)?(?:(?:#|--)(.*))?$/.exec(href || "");
    if (!parts) { return null; }
    return parts[2] ? parts[1] + "#" + parts[2] : parts[1];
  }

  // Show ITEM when its title or text holds WORDS, or any entry under it does.
  function show(item, words) {
    var link = item.querySelector(":scope > a");
    var title = link ? link.textContent.toLowerCase() : "";
    var text = (link && texts[keyOf(link.getAttribute("href"))]) || "";
    var hit = words === "" || title.indexOf(words) !== -1 || text.indexOf(words) !== -1;
    item.querySelectorAll(":scope > ol > li").forEach(function (inner) {
      if (show(inner, words)) { hit = true; }
    });
    item.classList.toggle("hidden", !hit);
    return hit;
  }

  box.addEventListener("input", function () {
    var words = box.value.trim().toLowerCase().replace(/\s+/g, " ");
    nav.classList.toggle("filtering", words !== "");
    nav.querySelectorAll("ol.chapters > li").forEach(function (item) { show(item, words); });
  });

  // The entry of the column for the part being read is marked, following as
  // the page is scrolled: the last heading that has reached the middle of
  // the window, and the entries it is under, so a section folded out of sight
  // leaves the section holding it marked. On the whole manual, where no chapter is marked as the one
  // shown, its chapter is unfolded too.
  var whole = !nav.querySelector("li.here");
  var here = location.pathname;
  var marks = [];
  nav.querySelectorAll("ol.chapters a").forEach(function (link) {
    var url = new URL(link.href, location.href);
    if (url.pathname !== here) { return; }
    var target = url.hash ? document.getElementById(decodeURIComponent(url.hash.slice(1)))
                          : document.querySelector("main h1");
    if (target) { marks.push({ link: link, target: target, top: 0 }); }
  });
  if (marks.length === 0) { return; }
  var marked = null;

  function measure() {
    marks.forEach(function (mark) { mark.top = mark.target.getBoundingClientRect().top + window.scrollY; });
    marks.sort(function (a, b) { return a.top - b.top; });
  }

  // Mark or unmark the entry of MARK and every entry it is under.
  function light(mark, on) {
    for (var item = mark.link.closest("li"); item && nav.contains(item); item = item.parentElement.closest("li")) {
      item.querySelector(":scope > a").classList.toggle("in-view", on);
    }
  }

  // Keep LINK in view in whichever part of the column scrolls.
  function reveal(link) {
    [nav, nav.querySelector("ol.chapters")].forEach(function (box) {
      if (!box || box.scrollHeight <= box.clientHeight) { return; }
      var outer = box.getBoundingClientRect();
      var inner = link.getBoundingClientRect();
      if (inner.top < outer.top) { box.scrollTop -= outer.top - inner.top + 24; }
      else if (inner.bottom > outer.bottom) { box.scrollTop += inner.bottom - outer.bottom + 24; }
    });
  }

  // Mark the entry of the last heading at or above the middle of the
  // window, the first when none is: a search in the measured places, the
  // page changed only when the entry is another.
  function follow() {
    var line = window.scrollY + window.innerHeight / 2;
    var low = 0, high = marks.length - 1, found = marks[0];
    while (low <= high) {
      var middle = (low + high) >> 1;
      if (marks[middle].top <= line) { found = marks[middle]; low = middle + 1; } else { high = middle - 1; }
    }
    if (found === marked) { return; }
    if (marked) { light(marked, false); }
    marked = found;
    light(marked, true);
    if (whole) {
      var chapter = marked.link.closest("ol.chapters > li");
      nav.querySelectorAll("ol.chapters > li.here").forEach(function (item) {
        if (item !== chapter) { item.classList.remove("here"); }
      });
      if (chapter) { chapter.classList.add("here"); }
    }
    reveal(marked.link);
  }

  // Measure again when pictures have loaded or the window changed size.
  function again() {
    measure();
    if (marked) { light(marked, false); }
    marked = null;
    follow();
  }

  measure();
  follow();
  window.addEventListener("scroll", follow, { passive: true });
  window.addEventListener("resize", again);
  window.addEventListener("load", again);
}());
